description:
Objective: stop a cleanup failure in archiveSource from destroying the release result it was supposed to leave untouched.

MEASURED DEFECT, verified on current bytes at scripts/release/supply-chain.mjs:58-65. `archiveSource` ends:

    } finally { rmSync(archive, { force: true }); }

with no try/catch around the rmSync. If that removal throws — Windows EBUSY on a held handle is the documented case — the throw escapes the `finally` and REPLACES whatever the try block was returning: a `{ destination, ok: true }` success, or an exact `SOURCE_ARCHIVE_FAILED` refusal. Either way the caller receives a raw filesystem exception instead of the release verdict that was actually computed.

THE FIX ALREADY EXISTS IN THIS FILE, 120 LINES BELOW. task-56117913 "Supply-chain failures must report their own reason" landed exactly this guard on `cleanRoots` (supply-chain.mjs:184-188), with a comment that states the rule verbatim:

    // Subordinate: a throw here escapes the caller's finally and REPLACES the real refusal
    // or success (Windows EBUSY on a held handle), so report each failure and keep removing
    // the remaining roots.

`cleanRoots` now wraps its `rmSync` calls in try/catch and logs each failure. `archiveSource` has the identical shape and did not get the identical treatment. This is the compound-finding half-fix pattern: one clause of a two-clause defect landed.

WHY A GREEN SUITE CANNOT SEE IT: the failure needs a real held handle on the archive file at the moment of removal, which is a Windows filesystem race. No test creates that condition, and the happy path exercises the same line thousands of times without incident.

WHY IT MATTERS ON THIS PROJECT SPECIFICALLY: this is the release supply-chain recorder, whose entire job is to report truthful release authority — its own header says "Success records UNKNOWN release authority, never publication." A cleanup exception masquerading as the release outcome is precisely the wrong-reason-code failure epic rail 4 exists to prevent, in the one script where a wrong answer is least acceptable.

PROVENANCE: raised by governor-18a42000's adversarial audit of today's diff, then re-verified line by line on current bytes by me before filing. One of two skeptics disputed it; I read the bytes myself and the shape is unambiguous.

NOT IN SCOPE: cleanRoots (already fixed), the release evidence format, the refusal vocabulary, or any other script under scripts/release/.

Verification: pnpm test:integration (tests/integration/release-supply-chain.test.mjs covers this script).
